			</div><div class="et_pb_section et_pb_section_3 et_section_regular">
				
				
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_row et_pb_row_10">
				<div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_4_4 et_pb_column_20 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough et-last-child">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_5 mini-head et_pb_text_align_center et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h2>What Causes Heating Problems? A Closer Look</h2></div>
			</div>
			</div>
				
				
				
				
			</div><div class="et_pb_row et_pb_row_11">
				<div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_1_2 et_pb_column_21 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_6 mini-head et_pb_text_align_left et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h3 style="text-align: center;">Ignition and Pilot Issues</h3>
<p>Failed igniters, pilot lights, flame sensors, and gas valves are among the most common reasons a furnace won’t start. These require a trained technician to diagnose and repair safely, since they involve gas and electrical components together.</p></div>
			</div>
			</div><div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_1_2 et_pb_column_22 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough et-last-child">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_7 mini-head et_pb_text_align_left et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h3 style="text-align: center;">Blower Motor Problems</h3>
<p>A failing blower motor causes weak airflow, no air circulation, or unusual noises from the system. Left unaddressed, it forces the rest of the system to work harder and wears out other components faster.</p></div>
			</div>
			</div>
				
				
				
				
			</div><div class="et_pb_row et_pb_row_12">
				<div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_1_2 et_pb_column_23 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_8 mini-head et_pb_text_align_left et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h3 style="text-align: center;">Thermostat Malfunctions</h3>
<p>A thermostat that isn’t reading temperature accurately can cause short cycling or a complete failure to heat, even when the furnace itself is otherwise healthy.</p></div>
			</div>
			</div><div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_1_2 et_pb_column_24 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough et-last-child">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_9 mini-head et_pb_text_align_left et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h3 style="text-align: center;">Dirty Filters and Restricted Airflow</h3>
<p>Dirty filters restrict airflow, forcing the system to work harder and driving up energy bills. Change filters every 1–2 months during heating season, and don’t block vents with furniture or curtains.</p></div>
			</div>
			</div>
				
				
				
				
			</div><div class="et_pb_row et_pb_row_13">
				<div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_4_4 et_pb_column_25 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough et-last-child">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_10 mini-head et_pb_text_align_left et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h3 style="text-align: center;">Cracked Heat Exchangers – A Safety Issue</h3>
<p>A cracked heat exchanger is a serious safety concern: it can leak carbon monoxide, a deadly gas, into your home. This requires immediate attention and often necessitates furnace <a href="/heating-service/">replacement</a> rather than repair. If you smell something unusual near your furnace or notice a yellow pilot flame, treat it as urgent.</p></div>
			</div>
			</div>
				
				
				
				
			</div>
				
				
			</div><div class="et_pb_section et_pb_section_4 et_pb_with_background et_section_regular">
				
				
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_row et_pb_row_14">
				<div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_4_4 et_pb_column_26 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough et-last-child">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_11 mini-head et_pb_text_align_left et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h2>How Much Does Heating Repair, Replacement, or Installation Cost?</h2>
<p style="text-align: left;">Costs vary widely depending on what’s wrong and what’s needed:</p>
<ul>
<li style="text-align: left;">Minor repairs (a filter or small sensor issue).</li>
<li style="text-align: left;">Larger component repairs (blower motor, heat exchanger, ignition system).</li>
<li style="text-align: left;">Full installation or replacement: depending on system type, size, efficiency rating, and installation complexity.</li>
</ul>
<p>We always provide a detailed, upfront quote before work begins. Ask about our <a href="/financing/">financing options</a> — flexible plans with low monthly payments and zero-interest same-as-cash programs are available for larger repairs and installations.</p></div>
			</div>
			</div>
				
				
				
				
			</div>
				
				
			</div><div class="et_pb_section et_pb_section_5 et_section_regular">
				
				
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_row et_pb_row_15">
				<div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_1_2 et_pb_column_27 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_12 mini-head et_pb_text_align_left et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h2>Repair or Replace? How to Decide</h2>
<p>A few honest questions help clarify the decision:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>How old is the system?</strong> Most furnaces last 15–20 years; if yours is older, no single repair will make it new again.</li>
<li><strong>How often are you calling for repairs?</strong> Once a year is normal wear and tear. Every few months is a sign to start planning for replacement.</li>
<li><strong>Are energy bills climbing?</strong> Rising bills with no change in usage usually mean the system is losing efficiency.</li>
<li><strong>Does it smell unusual or make strange noises?</strong> That’s an early warning sign the system is wearing out.</li>
<li><strong>Is the heat exchanger cracked?</strong> This is a safety issue on its own and typically points to replacement, regardless of the system’s age.</li>
</ul>
<p>As a general rule: consider replacement if your furnace is over 15 years old, needs frequent repairs, has lost efficiency, has a cracked heat exchanger, or repair costs exceed 50% of replacement value.</p></div>
			</div>
			</div><div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_1_2 et_pb_column_28 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough et-last-child">
				
				
				
				
